  "After midnight, your clerks reassemble every Essosi contract into one blackbound ledger and discover three incompatible obligations hidden by different brokers. One promises safe passage to your northern grain fleet. One promises priority seizure rights to Illyrio's creditors. The last grants a rival house option to buy out your captains if payments slip. Jorah calls it deliberate sabotage. Illyrio calls it layered risk pricing.

The hall is crowded with sleepless retainers who understand enough arithmetic to know that one of these promises must be broken before dawn. If you publish the conflict and force renegotiation in front of witnesses, you may preserve long legitimacy while absorbing immediate chaos. If you quietly void one clause through blackmail and forged addenda, the ships sail on schedule and every future pact becomes less trustworthy.

Wax drips onto your fingertips as you turn pages that already feel like evidence. This is where accounting becomes statecraft."

